   }
  },
  {
   "turn": 6,
   "response_text": "Perfect! I found the issue. Look at lines 192-193:\n\n```python\ninput_dim = X_train.shape[1]\ninput_dim = mcfg.get(\"input_dim\", input_dim)\n```\n\nThe actual data has shape (256, 105) where 105 is the real feature dimension, but the config specifies `\"input_dim\": 1`. This is incorrect. The code checks `mcfg.get(\"input_dim\", input_dim)` which means if `input_dim` is in the model config (which it is), it uses that value instead of the actual data dimension.\n\nThe config shows:\n```\n\"model\": {\"type\": \"mlp\", \"hidden_dims\": [64, 32], \"dropout\": 0.0, \"activation\": \"relu\", \"input_dim\": 1}\n```\n\nBut the actual training data has 105 features. When the first linear layer tries to multiply (256x105) with weights of shape (1x64), it fails.\n\nLet me check what the correct input_dim should be by looking at the datautil:",
   "tool_calls": [
    {
     "id": "toolu_011cnmAQR6sJaUT9fiXhoW6B",
     "name": "read_code",
     "arguments": {
      "path": "datautil.py"
